A||||5||DaveL||Timing Gear Blues||||5||
Z||000000||DaveL||07-02-2001||04:03 PM||||I'm looking at changing the timing gear on my 87 SE 2.5. Can it be done with the engine in the car? Also, do the nylon gears have any real advantage over the all steel gears?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Leadfoot||07-02-2001||05:37 PM||||If you raise the car and drop the back end of the cradle you should be able to get to it.  I think the back end can be dropped without disconnecting any hoses or electrical connections.  As for the gear material the steel gear will last the longest but it will be noisy.  I saw a site once that sold a brass/bronze gear which will run quieter.  The nylon gear will run quiet but will probably go out at around 100k.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||07-02-2001||07:40 PM||||Most people tell you the all metal gears last longer.... when you figure the original style gear set is 13-14 years old,,,,<P>Myself, I'd put in another OE style one. The engine will probably wear out before it quits a second time.||||reg||1
Z||000003||85Josh||07-02-2001||08:35 PM||||DaveL, check out this link:  <A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/timing-gear.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/timing-gear.html</A> <BR>I personally just removed enough stuff to let me get a die grinder in there and cut the old gear off. Of course, the clutch went out two weeks later, but thats another story.<BR>P.S. ogre, I agree with you on the fiber gear, the only reason I would use the metal one would be if I intended to use heavier valvesprings or a high-lift cam.<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4(Lil' Bit)<p>[This message has been edited by 85Josh (edited 07-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Rodrv6||07-02-2001||09:25 PM||||I was averaging about 65,000 miles between failures on the cam gear in my 84.  After the fourth time I had to change it I installed a set of all steel gears.  I can't tell any difference in the noise from the engine with the steel gears.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000005||Bob Englert||07-02-2001||09:34 PM||||I had the Fiero Factory replace the original gear with the metal one on my previous 85 SE.  I really didn't notice any additional noise with the metal gear.  I was driving the car all over the Carolinas and didn't want it to die somewhere in the middle of nowhere.<P>When I get around to restoring the white 85 SE resting at Ed's storage yard, I plan to have Grooms rebuild the original engine and install a metal gear.  For about $25 more I will never have to worry about it.<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl<p>[This message has been edited by Bob Englert (edited 07-03-2001).]||||reg||1
